kosha architecture-varga

  -- architecture: the discipline of organising space for human habitation and use.
  -- architecture sits at the intersection of:
  --   engineering:  structural integrity, material performance, building systems.
  --   geometry:     space, form, proportion, symmetry, light.
  --   human factors: scale, comfort, circulation, acoustics, thermal comfort.
  --   culture:      meaning, symbol, beauty, identity.
  --
  -- the architect's question is always dual:
  --   will it stand? (engineering — gravity, load, material)
  --   does it serve? (human — space, light, movement, meaning)
  --
  -- the three Vitruvian principles (1st century BC, still the foundation):
  --   firmitas:   structural stability. it must stand.
  --   utilitas:   functional utility. it must work.
  --   venustas:   beauty. it must delight.
  --
  -- scale: architecture deals with the human body as the reference.
  --   doorway: ~2.1m (must clear a standing person with headroom).
  --   corridor: ~1.2m minimum (two people passing), 1.8m comfortable.
  --   ceiling height: 2.4m minimum, 3m+ for public spaces.
  --   step riser: 150-180mm. tread: 250-300mm (comfortable stride).
  --   Le Corbusier's Modulor: human body proportions as design module.
  --
  -- structural systems in architecture:
  --   bearing wall: walls carry vertical load. oldest system. masonry, brick.
  --   frame:        columns and beams carry load. walls are non-structural (curtain).
  --                 steel frame → glass curtain wall → modern skyscraper possible.
  --   shell:        curved surface carries load by form (arch, dome, vault).
  --                 efficient: thin shell spans large spaces with minimal material.
  --   tension:      cables, membranes. tent structures. suspension roofs.
  --
  -- the building envelope: the boundary between inside and outside.
  --   roof: keeps rain out. manages thermal load. drainage.
  --   walls: vertical enclosure. thermal insulation. structural or curtain.
  --   glazing: light + view + thermal loss. U-value measures insulation.
  --   floor: structural support + thermal mass + finish.

  "engineering-varga-vishesa"
  "subanta-swarupa"

